   "subject": "Coding SEM from graphical representation",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"></p><div style=\"text-align:left\">Good day, </div><div style=\"text-align:left\">I&#39;m attempting to code and test a SEM from a graphical representation of my study environment. The ultimate goal to find what environmental factors have the greatest influence on berry production (nBerries) with flower production (nFlwrs) being a critical first step to berry growth. </div><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Sih6yAufiAw/Ws0bCUUn_gI/AAAAAAAAA3Q/plq-C0DieXoNO87GkmUqra2o_iYpOBoKQCLcBGAs/s1600/SEM_Across.png\" style=\"clear:left;margin-bottom:1em;float:left;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Sih6yAufiAw/Ws0bCUUn_gI/AAAAAAAAA3Q/plq-C0DieXoNO87GkmUqra2o_iYpOBoKQCLcBGAs/s1600/SEM_Across.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792425747177000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3LezirFaVz2OPC51qGhf4X\"><br></a><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Sih6yAufiAw/Ws0bCUUn_gI/AAAAAAAAA3Q/plq-C0DieXoNO87GkmUqra2o_iYpOBoKQCLcBGAs/s1600/SEM_Across.png\" style=\"clear:left;margin-bottom:1em;float:left;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Sih6yAufiAw/Ws0bCUUn_gI/AAAAAAAAA3Q/plq-C0DieXoNO87GkmUqra2o_iYpOBoKQCLcBGAs/s1600/SEM_Across.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792425747177000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3LezirFaVz2OPC51qGhf4X\"><img src=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Sih6yAufiAw/Ws0bCUUn_gI/AAAAAAAAA3Q/plq-C0DieXoNO87GkmUqra2o_iYpOBoKQCLcBGAs/s320/SEM_Across.png\" border=\"0\" width=\"320\" height=\"115\"></a><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Sih6yAufiAw/Ws0bCUUn_gI/AAAAAAAAA3Q/plq-C0DieXoNO87GkmUqra2o_iYpOBoKQCLcBGAs/s1600/SEM_Across.png\" style=\"clear:left;margin-bottom:1em;float:left;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Sih6yAufiAw/Ws0bCUUn_gI/AAAAAAAAA3Q/plq-C0DieXoNO87GkmUqra2o_iYpOBoKQCLcBGAs/s1600/SEM_Across.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792425747177000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3LezirFaVz2OPC51qGhf4X\"><br></a><p></p><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;ve hit a few small snags </div><div> </div><div><b>1)  How best to code indirect effects?</b><i> eg. Age on Canopy Cover</i></div><div><i> </i>At the moment I&#39;m representing nearly all the relationships through regression</div><div><div><br></div><div>library(lavaan) </div><div>Across1 &lt;-&#39;</div><div>#regressions</div><div>TFR.vvi ~ AvTemp.vvi</div><div>Pollen.vvi ~ AvTemp.vvi</div><div>CanCov.vvi ~ Age.vvi</div><div>nFlwrs.vvi ~ ALD.vvi + CanCov.vvi + Age.vvi</div><div>nBerries.vvi ~ Pollen.vvi + TFR.vvi + AvTemp.vvi + AvMoist.vvi + nFlwrs.vvi</div><div><br></div><div># residual correlations</div><div>ALD.vvi~~CanCov.vvi</div><div>&#39; </div><div><br></div><div>fit=sem(Across1, data=SEMtest, missing=&#39;FIML&#39;) </div><div>summary(fit,standardized=T) </div></div><div><div><i><br></i></div></div><div><b>2)  After running the model I get a few errors</b></div><div><pre style=\"outline:none;border-width:initial;border-style:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:16px;font-variant-numeric:normal;font-variant-east-asian:normal\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;font-size:10.4pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important;color:rgb(197,6,11)\">Warning messages:\r\n</span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;font-size:10.4pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important;color:rgb(197,6,11)\">1: </span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;font-size:10.4pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important;color:rgb(197,6,11)\">In lav_data_full(data = data, group = group, cluster = cluster,  :\r\n</span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;font-size:10.4pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important;color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> </span><span><font color=\"#c5060b\" face=\"Ubuntu Mono\"><span style=\"font-size:10.4pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"> lavaan WARNING: some observed variances are (at least) a factor 1000 times larger than others; use varTable(fit) to investigate</span></font></span></pre>Some of my variables are the same across many samples (eg. Age) while others are not (eg. Pollen) so this makes sense to me. <br>Once I get this model sorted I have simpler models to look at local level effects and to account for any potential problems from pseudo-replication. </div><div><br><pre style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;outline:none;border-width:initial;border-style:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:16px;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-variant-numeric:normal;font-variant-east-asian:normal;font-size:10.4pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">2: </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">In lavaan::lavaan(model = Across1, data = SEMtest, missing = &quot;FIML&quot;,  :\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> lavaan WARNING: syntax contains parameters involving exogenous covariates; switching to fixed.x = FALSE</span></pre>This second error I am less clear on the meaning.<pre style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;outline:none;border-width:initial;border-style:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:16px;font-variant-numeric:normal;font-variant-east-asian:normal;font-size:10.4pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><span><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></span></pre><div><br></div>My sincerest thanks for any insight. <br>Lindsey<br></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
