     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Lindsey,<div>See below.<br><div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Tue, Apr 10, 2018 at 3:57 PM, Lindsey Parkinson <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">lvpar...@alaska.edu</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"></p><div style=\"text-align:left\">Good day, </div><div style=\"text-align:left\">I&#39;m attempting to code and test a SEM from a graphical representation of my study environment. The ultimate goal to find what environmental factors have the greatest influence on berry production (nBerries) with flower production (nFlwrs) being a critical first step to berry growth. </div><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Sih6yAufiAw/Ws0bCUUn_gI/AAAAAAAAA3Q/plq-C0DieXoNO87GkmUqra2o_iYpOBoKQCLcBGAs/s1600/SEM_Across.png\" style=\"clear:left;margin-bottom:1em;float:left;margin-right:1em\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Sih6yAufiAw/Ws0bCUUn_gI/AAAAAAAAA3Q/plq-C0DieXoNO87GkmUqra2o_iYpOBoKQCLcBGAs/s1600/SEM_Across.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792425747184000&amp;usg=AOvVaw04FTS_4AuVoQgzfmiuvxJ0\"><br></a><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Sih6yAufiAw/Ws0bCUUn_gI/AAAAAAAAA3Q/plq-C0DieXoNO87GkmUqra2o_iYpOBoKQCLcBGAs/s1600/SEM_Across.png\" style=\"clear:left;margin-bottom:1em;float:left;margin-right:1em\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Sih6yAufiAw/Ws0bCUUn_gI/AAAAAAAAA3Q/plq-C0DieXoNO87GkmUqra2o_iYpOBoKQCLcBGAs/s1600/SEM_Across.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792425747184000&amp;usg=AOvVaw04FTS_4AuVoQgzfmiuvxJ0\"><img src=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Sih6yAufiAw/Ws0bCUUn_gI/AAAAAAAAA3Q/plq-C0DieXoNO87GkmUqra2o_iYpOBoKQCLcBGAs/s320/SEM_Across.png\" border=\"0\" width=\"320\" height=\"115\"></a><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Sih6yAufiAw/Ws0bCUUn_gI/AAAAAAAAA3Q/plq-C0DieXoNO87GkmUqra2o_iYpOBoKQCLcBGAs/s1600/SEM_Across.png\" style=\"clear:left;margin-bottom:1em;float:left;margin-right:1em\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Sih6yAufiAw/Ws0bCUUn_gI/AAAAAAAAA3Q/plq-C0DieXoNO87GkmUqra2o_iYpOBoKQCLcBGAs/s1600/SEM_Across.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792425747184000&amp;usg=AOvVaw04FTS_4AuVoQgzfmiuvxJ0\"><br></a><p></p><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;ve hit a few small snags </div><div> </div><div><b>1)  How best to code indirect effects?</b><i> eg. Age on Canopy Cover</i></div><div><i> </i>At the moment I&#39;m representing nearly all the relationships through regression</div><div><div><br></div><div>library(lavaan) </div><div>Across1 &lt;-&#39;</div><div>#regressions</div><div>TFR.vvi ~ AvTemp.vvi</div><div>Pollen.vvi ~ AvTemp.vvi</div><div>CanCov.vvi ~ Age.vvi</div><div>nFlwrs.vvi ~ ALD.vvi + CanCov.vvi + Age.vvi</div><div>nBerries.vvi ~ Pollen.vvi + TFR.vvi + AvTemp.vvi + AvMoist.vvi + nFlwrs.vvi</div><div><br></div><div># residual correlations</div><div>ALD.vvi~~CanCov.vvi</div><div>&#39; </div><div><br></div><div>fit=sem(Across1, data=SEMtest, missing=&#39;FIML&#39;) </div><div>summary(fit,standardized=T) </div></div><div><div><i><br></i></div></div><div><b>2)  After running the model I get a few errors</b></div><div><pre style=\"outline:none;border-width:initial;border-style:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:16px\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;color:rgb(197,6,11);font-size:10.4pt;white-space:pre-wrap\">Warning messages:\r\n</span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;color:rgb(197,6,11);font-size:10.4pt;white-space:pre-wrap\">1: </span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;color:rgb(197,6,11);font-size:10.4pt;white-space:pre-wrap\">In lav_data_full(data = data, group = group, cluster = cluster,  :\r\n</span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;color:rgb(197,6,11);font-size:10.4pt;white-space:pre-wrap\"> </span><span><font color=\"#c5060b\" face=\"Ubuntu Mono\"><span style=\"font-size:10.4pt;white-space:pre-wrap\"> lavaan WARNING: some observed variances are (at least) a factor 1000 times larger than others; use varTable(fit) to investigate</span></font></span></pre>Some of my variables are the same across many samples (eg. Age) while others are not (eg. Pollen) so this makes sense to me. <br>Once I get this model sorted I have simpler models to look at local level effects and to account for any potential problems from pseudo-replication. </div><div><br></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>Look at varTable(fit) and you will see that one (or more) of your variables have very large variances relative to other variables. Standardizing this particular variable(s), if it makes sense, would make this message go away. Variables with very widely different variances can be problematic in SEM and path analysis.</div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><pre style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;outline:none;border-width:initial;border-style:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:16px;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-size:10.4pt;white-space:pre-wrap\"><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">2: </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">In lavaan::lavaan(model = Across1, data = SEMtest, missing = &quot;FIML&quot;,  :\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> lavaan WARNING: syntax contains parameters involving exogenous covariates; switching to fixed.x = FALSE</span></pre>This second error I am less clear on the meaning.<pre style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;outline:none;border-width:initial;border-style:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:16px;font-size:10.4pt;white-space:pre-wrap\"><span><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></span></pre></div></div></blockquote><div>This is because of this line of code:</div><div> </div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;font-style:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);float:none;display:inline\"><div style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;font-style:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"># residual correlations</div><div style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;font-style:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\">ALD.vvi~~CanCov.vvi</div></span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;font-style:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);float:none;display:inline\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;font-style:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);float:none;display:inline\">ALD.vvi and CanCov.vvi are both covariates and not dependent variables in your model. Therefore, they are considered fixed and not random variables. When you estimate this covariance, they are now considered random and that&#39;s why lavaan is giving you that message. Given your comment that says &quot;residual correlations&quot;, I wonder if you meant to have something else covary?</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;font-style:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);float:none;display:inline\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;font-style:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);float:none;display:inline\">For coding indirect effects, this tutorial should help: <a href=\"http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/mediation.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/mediation.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792425747184000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1SQLMjerCCilF08VcHKZFz\">http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/mediation.html</a></span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;font-style:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);float:none;display:inline\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;font-style:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);float:none;display:inline\">Best,</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;font-style:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);float:none;display:inline\">Chris</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:small;font-style:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);float:none;display:inline\"><br></span></div></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div>"
